<p>For a closer look at the lead side, read <a href="/posts/ai-lead-generation-for-personal-trainers/">AI lead generation for personal trainers</a>.</p>
<h2 id="setup-1-brand-files">Setup 1: Brand files</h2>
<p>AI gives generic answers when it does not know you. The fix is a small set of documents about your business. Your AI tool reads them before it answers anything.</p>
<p>Here is what goes in each one:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Client-context file:</strong> holds who you train, what you sell and what your clients struggle with. Not sure what to write? The free <a href="/tools/find-your-ideal-clients-faqs-for-coaches/">Ideal Client FAQ Finder</a> asks you six questions about the clients you want and builds your ideal-client profile from your answers.</li>
<li><strong>Voice-and-writing file:</strong> holds how you talk. Paste in three to five captions, emails or replies you have actually written. AI copies patterns far better than it follows adjectives, and &quot;friendly and professional&quot; means nothing to it. Then add a &quot;do&quot; list and a &quot;never use&quot; list for words, phrases and structures. Put your hard rules at the top, such as: never invent a result, testimonial or client story, and if you do not know something, say so and ask me.</li>
<li><strong>Branding</strong> <strong>file:</strong> holds your colors, fonts and visual style.</li>
<li><strong>Brand-reference</strong> <strong>file</strong>: samples of your best work (client program and testimonial, or best-performing content), so AI can see what good looks like.</li>
<li><strong>CONTENT-CONFIG file:</strong> points AI to the right brand folder. Think of this as your AI's table of contents. It reads it first and knows which document to read next. See below.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="brand-files-structure-for-ai">Brand files structure for AI</h3>
<p>Create these files and save them in your Claude project with this hierarchy:</p>
<pre class="file-tree"><code>Client Content System/
├── CONTENT-CONFIG.md
└── Brand/
    ├── client-context.md
    ├── voice-and-writing.md
    ├── branding.md
    └── brand-reference.pdf</code></pre>
<p>The .md ending stands for markdown. It is just a plain text file, so you can write it in any text editor, or ask Claude to turn your notes into one.</p>
<h3 id="where-the-brand-files-live-in-your-ai-tool">Where the brand files live in your AI tool</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>In Claude:</strong> Upload your brand files in the project's files section (called &quot;Project knowledge&quot;). In the project instructions box, add one line such as: &quot;Read all files in the Brand folder before answering anything.&quot;</li>
<li><strong>In ChatGPT:</strong> it works the same way. A Project has an instructions box and a files area, so upload the same files there.</li>
<li>Your brand files stay the same across everything you do. If you use more than one Project, such as content, client replies and leads, upload the same brand files into each one. Then give each Project its own short instructions for that job.</li>
<li>Keep a master copy in Google Drive or on your device. Gen AI tools can read files from there too. When you update a file, re-upload it to each Project so they all stay in sync.</li>
</ul>
<p>You only do this once. After that, you never type who you are again. The project supplies your details automatically, every time.</p>
<aside class="quick-check"><h3 id="how-to-test-brand-files">How to test it</h3>
<ul>
<li>Give it three real tasks you have already done, such as a reply to a lead, an Instagram caption and a short email. Compare each draft to what you actually sent.</li>
<li>When something sounds off, fix the file, not the chat, because a correction in the chat disappears next time. Repeat until you would send the draft with light edits.</li>
</ul>
</aside>
<h3>Why it matters</h3>
<p>Gen AI tools are <strong>built to always give an answer, even when they are unsure</strong>. That is why they often make up names, data, ideas and offers about you. Give them your files, with a rule like &quot;stick to this and nothing else,&quot; and they will stay inside what you wrote.</p>
<h2 id="setup-2-offer-plan">Setup 2: Offer plan</h2>
<p>Your brand files tell AI who you are. The offer plan tells it what you sell. It is one document that covers everything in your catalog, so AI always knows what you offer and how you sell it.</p>
<p>A service line is a main thing you sell, such as 1:1 coaching, a group challenge or an online program. You might have three or just one <a href="/posts/signature-fitness-coaching-offer/">signature program</a>. Either way, write it down once. Your emails, ads and follow-ups can all be built on it.</p>
<p>Here is what goes in for each service line:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>What it is:</strong> The offer, the length and the price.</li>
<li><strong>What is included, and what is not:</strong> The &quot;not included&quot; line matters. Without it, AI tends to oversell and promise things you do not offer.</li>
<li><strong>Who it is for, and who it is not for:</strong> Name the client it fits best. Then name who you turn down and why. If AI cannot say no to a bad fit, this part is too thin.</li>
<li><strong>Where it goes next:</strong> What you are pushing this month and what comes after.</li>
</ul>
<p>Then add three things that help with replies and marketing:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Why someone picks you:</strong> Write one sentence. This is the line AI uses when it writes about your offers.</li>
<li><strong>Your follow-up rhythm:</strong> For example, what you send on day 1, 3, 7 and 14 after a consult, and the angle of each message.</li>
<li><strong>Objections you hear often:</strong> Write two or three answers, such as for &quot;it costs too much,&quot; &quot;I will start next month&quot; or &quot;I tried a trainer before.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>Already have emails, sales pages or DMs that show what you sell and how you sell it? Paste them in too. Real examples teach AI more than descriptions do.</p>
<pre class="file-tree"><code>offer-plan.md
├── Rule: if you are unsure, list what is missing and ask me before you write
├── Service line 1: 1:1 coaching
│   ├── What it is
│   ├── Included / not included
│   ├── Who it is for / not for
│   └── Where it goes next
├── Service line 2: Group challenge (same four parts)
├── Service line 3: Online program (same four parts)
├── Shared: why someone picks you
├── Shared: follow-up rhythm
├── Shared: objections and answers
└── Examples: real emails, sales pages or DMs</code></pre>
<h3 id="where-offer-plan-lives">Where it lives</h3>
<ul>
<li>Add it as one more file next to your brand files. Upload it to your Project and keep a master copy in Drive or on your device, the same way as in Setup 1.</li>
<li>Add one rule at the top: if you are unsure about something, list what is missing and ask me before you write.</li>
</ul>
<aside class="quick-check"><h3 id="how-to-test-offer-plan">How to test it</h3>
<ul>
<li>Paste in a messy note from a real consult and ask for a <a href="/posts/follow-up-for-trainers/">follow-up message</a>, plus which service line fits that person. Then describe a bad-fit lead and ask whether you should take them. If it says yes to everyone, add more detail to your &quot;who it is not for&quot; section.</li>
</ul>
</aside>
<h3>Why it matters</h3>
<p>This file is what lets you use AI for the work around each service line. Ask for five ways to introduce your challenge, ad text for one offer or a three-email sequence. The answers fit what you actually sell, because it is all in the plan. It also stops AI from making up promises, so you save time without risking your reputation.</p>
<h3>Where you decide</h3>
<p>You choose what to sell, what it costs and what you promise. Check every claim before anything goes out.</p>
<h2 id="setup-3-content-repurposing">Setup 3: Content repurposing</h2>
<p>This setup is your repurposer and distributor. You create one main piece of content, and AI turns it into posts for every platform you use. It also helps you plan what goes out and when.</p>
<h3 id="the-logic-behind-a-content-repurposing-workflow-for-personal-trainers">The logic behind a content repurposing workflow for personal trainers</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>One main piece:</strong> You create the one piece everything else is built from, such as a video, a blog post, a newsletter or a voice note. Which one depends on how you already create.</li>
<li><strong>A workflow that picks the format first:</strong> Before AI writes anything, it decides which platform and format suits each idea. This step stops you from forcing every idea into every platform.</li>
<li><strong>One file per platform you use:</strong> Each platform gets its own file with the spec for that platform and samples of your best posts there. Include only the platforms you actually use, and make sure every one of them has a file, so none gets skipped.</li>
<li><strong>Your brand files underneath:</strong> Your voice, branding and brand reference from Setup 1 apply to every platform, so every version sounds like you and not like generic fitness copy.</li>
<li><strong>Your review at the end:</strong> AI drafts, you decide. Nothing goes out unread.</li>
</ul>
<p>The exact steps depend on how you create and how often you post, so build the workflow for your own business. What stays the same is the order: AI extracts from your piece first, you review that, and only then does it write.</p>
<p>Two rules belong in every setup:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stick to your piece:</strong> Tell AI that every claim, quote and number must come from your piece. Anything that is not in it gets left out.</li>
<li><strong>Keep your opinion:</strong> Tell it to keep your actual point of view, so a sharp take does not get smoothed into generic advice.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="content-repurposing-structure">Content repurposing structure</h3>
<p>Create it and save it in your Claude project:</p>
<pre class="file-tree"><code>Client Content System/
├── 00_START-HERE.md
├── AI-RULES.md
├── CONTENT-WORKFLOW.md
│   └── choose-platform-format
│       └── PLATFORM-FORMAT-RULES.md
├── CONTENT-PACK-SPEC.md
├── DISTRIBUTION.md
├── QA-CHECKLIST.md
├── offer-plan.md
├── Platforms/
│   ├── instagram.md
│   ├── youtube.md
│   ├── x.md
│   └── (one file for each platform you use)
└── Brand/
    ├── client-context.md
    ├── voice-and-writing.md
    ├── branding.md
    └── brand-reference.pdf
CLAUDE.md ─┐
           ├── shared system above
AGENTS.md ─┘</code></pre>
<p>Here is what goes in each one:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>00_START-HERE:</strong> Tells AI what this system is and which file to read for which job.</li>
<li><strong>AI-RULES:</strong> Your hard rules, such as &quot;every claim, quote and number must come from my piece&quot; and &quot;keep my opinion.&quot;</li>
<li><strong>CONTENT-WORKFLOW:</strong> The steps you and AI follow from main piece to finished posts, built around how you work.</li>
<li><strong>PLATFORM-FORMAT-RULES:</strong> How AI decides which platform and format fits each idea.</li>
<li><strong>CONTENT-PACK-SPEC:</strong> What one main piece should produce, such as a carousel, two scripts and an email.</li>
<li><strong>Platforms folder:</strong> One file per platform you use. Each one holds the spec (length, structure, what to leave out) plus samples of your best posts on that platform.</li>
<li><strong>DISTRIBUTION:</strong> What goes out where, and when.</li>
<li><strong>QA-CHECKLIST:</strong> What you check before anything is posted.</li>
<li><strong>CLAUDE and AGENTS:</strong> Some tools look for a specific file name. These two just point to the same system, so you only maintain one set of files.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="platformbased-workflow">Platform-based workflow:</h3>
<div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>What AI drafts</th><th>Example spec</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Instagram</td><td>A carousel with a caption</td><td>One point per slide, a short caption that ends with a question</td></tr><tr><td>Reels, TikTok or Shorts</td><td>A short video script</td><td>A hook in the first line, one idea, under 30 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>LinkedIn</td><td>A text post in your voice</td><td>A hook, three short points, one call to action, no hashtags</td></tr><tr><td>Email</td><td>A short newsletter version</td><td>One takeaway, one story, one link</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>For example, you record a ten-minute video on whether clients can keep lifting while their knees feel stiff. AI picks the right format for each platform, you check the outline, and it drafts a carousel, two scripts, a LinkedIn post and an email. That is one afternoon of work feeding a whole week of posting.</p>
<h3 id="where-content-files-live">Where it lives</h3>
<ul>
<li>Upload the files to your Project, the same way as in Setup 1 and 2. Keep a master copy in Drive or on your device.</li>
<li>Tools that work with folders on your computer read CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md first. If you only use a Project, you can skip those two.</li>
</ul>
<aside class="quick-check"><h3 id="how-to-test-content-repurposing">How to test it</h3>
<p>Run one real piece through your workflow and check three things.</p>
<ul>
<li>Can you trace every quote back to your original?</li>
<li>Does each post make one point instead of summarizing the whole piece?</li>
<li>Would you post it after a voice edit only?</li>
</ul>
<p>Expect a strong first draft, not something you can publish without reading.</p>
</aside>
<h3 id="where-you-decide">Where you decide</h3>
<p>You choose which piece is worth repurposing and which platforms fit you. You also read every draft before it goes out. If a post does not sound like something you would say, change it or drop it.</p>
<h2 id="setup-4-lead-followup">Setup 4: Lead follow-up</h2>
<p>New messages may arrive at bad times, like mid-session or late at night. In this setup, AI does the first pass and you make the call. That is what semi-automated means here.</p>
<p><strong>One limit first:</strong> AI does not find leads for you, and it only knows the leads you give it. Your leads come from your DMs, your referrals, your past inquiries and people who already know you. Once you have a real list, AI can do four jobs:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Qualify:</strong> AI reads a new inquiry and checks it against your list of qualifying questions, such as goal, timeline and past experience. It tells you which answers are already in the message and which are missing, and drafts the questions to ask next.</li>
<li><strong>Sort:</strong> AI scores each lead using your scoring rule and puts it in one of three groups: ready to book, needs a nudge or not a fit. Each lead gets a score, a one-sentence reason and a fit flag.</li>
<li><strong>Draft:</strong> AI writes a first reply and a follow-up in your voice, using your brand files and the follow-up rhythm from your offer plan. No lead waits a week because you were busy.</li>
<li><strong>Remind:</strong> AI cannot track time on its own. Paste in your lead list with the date of your last message to each person, and AI shows you who has gone quiet. It then drafts the nudge for each one.<strong></strong></li>
</ul>
<h3 id="the-flow-step-by-step">The flow, step by step</h3>
<p><strong>1. A new message comes in. Paste it in.</strong> AI does the qualify and sort step in one pass. It checks the message against your questions, scores it using your rule and gives a flag. It also lists what is still missing.</p>
<p>Here is an example with a made-up lead: <em>&quot;Hi, saw your page. I want to lose weight before my wedding in 3 months. How much is it?&quot;</em> Using example points (clear goal +2, wants to start within a month +2, asked about price or booking +2, has trained before +1, fits your location or online +1; flag anything outside your scope):</p>
<ul>
<li>Score: 5 out of 8. Clear goal (+2), asked about price (+2), fits online (+1).</li>
<li>Flag: needs a nudge.</li>
<li>Missing: when they want to start, and whether they've trained before.</li>
<li>Reason: one sentence.</li>
</ul>
<p>Flags would be: 6 to 8 ready to book, 3 to 5 needs a nudge, 0 to 2 or a scope flag not a fit. Adjust the points to the clients you have actually signed.</p>
<p><strong>2. Draft.</strong> AI writes a reply in your voice using your brand files, your offer plan and your reply examples. For that lead, it would answer the price question from your offer plan and ask the two missing questions. If the lead mentions an injury, AI should stop and tell you, not draft.</p>
<p><strong>3. You edit and send.</strong> Read it, fix anything that doesn't sound like you, and send it yourself. Talk to ready-to-book leads in person. Then update the log.</p>
<p><strong>4. Weekly check (this is the &quot;remind&quot; job).</strong> Paste your lead log and ask: &quot;Who is due a follow-up under my rhythm, and what should I say to each?&quot; It lists the leads that have gone quiet and drafts the nudge for each. Without the pasted log, it can't remind you of anything.</p>
<pre class="file-tree"><code>└── Leads/
    ├── LEAD-RULES.md
    │   ├── Rule: draft only, never send. If unsure, list what's missing and ask me
    │   ├── Questions to ask every new lead (goal, timeline, past experience)
    │   ├── Scoring rule (points per answer)
    │   ├── Fit flags: ready to book / needs a nudge / not a fit (what each means)
    │   └── Escalate to me, don't draft: injuries or medical issues, refunds, complaints
    ├── lead-log (a sheet or note you keep)
    │   └── Name | Source | First message date | Last contact | Score | Flag | Next step
    └── reply-examples.md
        └── Real replies you've sent: first reply, nudge, ready-to-book, polite no</code></pre>
<aside class="quick-check"><h3 id="how-to-test-lead-follow-up">How to test it</h3>
<ul>
<li>Take ten past inquiries where you know what happened.</li>
<li>Score them without telling AI the outcome, then compare.</li>
<li>If it calls a lead who ghosted you &quot;ready to book,&quot; your points are too loose.</li>
</ul>
</aside>
<p>For the full follow-up system, read <a href="/posts/follow-up-for-trainers/">the follow-up guide for trainers</a>.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-tell-whether-each-setup-is-working">How to tell whether each setup is working</h2>
<p>Test each setup once when you build it, using the quick tests above. After that, you do not need a dashboard. You need one signal per setup that you can check in a few minutes each week.</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Setup</th><th>Signal to watch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Brand files</td><td>Do AI drafts sound like you, with only small edits needed?</td></tr><tr><td>Offer plan</td><td>Do new inquiries ask about the offer you are promoting?</td></tr><tr><td>Content flow</td><td>How many hours does a week of content take now, compared with before?</td></tr><tr><td>Lead follow-up</td><td>How fast do you reply, and how many consults get booked from the leads you receive?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Read the signals together. Drafts that need heavy rewrites point at Setup 1, because AI still does not know you well enough. Plenty of views but few inquiries point at Setup 2, because people see you but do not know what to ask for. Many inquiries but few bookings point at Setup 4.</p>
<p>Change one setup at a time, so you know which change made the difference.</p>
<h2 id="what-this-costs-you-and-where-it-goes-wrong">What this costs you and where it goes wrong</h2>
<ul class="cost-list">
  <li><strong>Allow a few focused hours for setup.</strong> Writing your brand files and offer plan takes time now and saves repetitive work later. Expect the first drafts to need editing; use the first week to improve the files.</li>
  <li><strong>Specific inputs make better drafts.</strong> Vague notes such as &quot;friendly and professional&quot; produce vague copy. Add real writing examples and clear lines about who you do not serve.</li>
  <li><strong>Start with what you need.</strong> If you have only a handful of clients, Setups 1 and 4 may be enough. Add the offer plan and content flow when you have time and an offer that already sells.</li>
</ul>
<aside class="takeaway"><p><strong>Keep the expectation realistic.</strong> None of this guarantees more clients. AI saves you time and keeps you consistent. The trust that turns a lead into a client still comes from you.</p></aside>
<h2 id="personal-trainer-marketing-faq">Personal trainer marketing FAQ</h2>
<h3 id="how-do-personal-trainers-market-themselves-with-no-budget">How do personal trainers market themselves with no budget?</h3>
<p>Start with free channels you control. Define who you train, message the people who already know you, and turn the questions clients ask into posts. Reply quickly to every inquiry. Your main cost is time, so choose one or two activities and repeat them every week.</p>
<h3 id="how-many-marketing-channels-should-a-personal-trainer-use-at-once">How many marketing channels should a personal trainer use at once?</h3>
<p>Fewer than you think. Most solo trainers do better with one channel for reaching new people and one for staying in touch, such as Instagram plus email. Add another only when you can keep both current without strain. Consistency matters more than coverage.</p>
<h3 id="do-you-need-a-website-or-is-instagram-enough">Do you need a website, or is Instagram enough?</h3>
<p>Instagram helps people find you, but a simple website gives you a place you control. It should say who you help, what you offer and how to book. If you can only build one first, pick the place your next client is most likely to look, then add the other.</p>
<h3 id="can-personal-trainers-use-ai-for-marketing-without-hurting-client-trust">Can personal trainers use AI for marketing without hurting client trust?</h3>
<p>Yes, if you keep a human in the loop. Let AI draft, then edit and send every message yourself. Never let it invent results or testimonials, and keep client health details out of it. Clients trust you, not the tool, so the final voice must be yours.</p>
<h3 id="what-if-ai-still-sounds-generic-after-i-set-it-up">What if AI still sounds generic after I set it up?</h3>
<p>Fix the files, not the chat. Add three to five real examples of your writing to your voice file, add a list of words and phrases you never use, and run the three-task test again. Generic answers almost always mean AI still does not have enough of you to work from.</p>
<h3 id="can-ai-find-me-new-clients-or-leads">Can AI find me new clients or leads?</h3>
<p>No. It can sort, prioritize and draft replies once you give it real leads, but it cannot find them. If you ask it for a list of people to contact, it may make up names. Start with the people who already know you, your DMs and your referrals.</p>
<h2 id="pick-one-setup-and-start">Pick one setup and start</h2>
<p>Brand files, an offer plan, a content flow and lead follow-up work best together, with AI doing the drafting and you making the decisions. Start with the one that fixes your biggest frustration. If you are not sure who you want to train, the free <a href="/tools/find-your-ideal-clients-faqs-for-coaches/">Ideal Client FAQ Finder</a> is a good place to begin.</p>
